{"reference":"1 Corinthians 16","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":16,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter closes the letter with practical arrangements: a planned gift, travel plans, care for coworkers, recognition of service, greetings between assemblies, and final commands shaped by love. Its many names and movements show a network of communities rather than an isolated congregation.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:10-19","1 Corinthians 16:22","1 Corinthians 16:5-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_4079760fa134a58a9c3ef35c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"The Collection","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The collection is deliberately organized. Each person is to set something aside regularly in proportion to prosperity, so that no urgent collection is needed when Paul arrives. The Corinthians are to approve the representatives who will carry the gift with letters, and Paul may accompany them if appropriate. This arrangement gives the donor community a role in the gift’s custody rather than placing its handling under one leader’s unilateral control.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:1-2","1 Corinthians 16:2","1 Corinthians 16:3-4"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_662c914967d81ded61902b15"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"people_places","title":"Travel and Coworkers","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s plans are conditional: he hopes to remain at Ephesus because of an open opportunity despite opposition, and he hopes later to spend time in Corinth if permitted. Apollos, though strongly urged to visit, does not wish to go at that time and will come when he has opportunity. The passage therefore portrays shared work involving opportunity, opposition, planning, and a coworker’s freedom to decline a proposed itinerary.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:12","1 Corinthians 16:19","1 Corinthians 16:5-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_ea74ed371701f9b15d0756fc"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Timothy is not treated merely as a messenger. Paul asks the Corinthians to ensure that he has no reason for fear among them and to send him onward in peace. Likewise, Stephanas, Fortunatus, and Achaicus are recognized for service that refreshed both Paul and the Corinthians. The chapter asks the community to honor labor that sustains its shared life.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:10-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_cf70b66211e7982494bd21db"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Prisca and Aquila greet the Corinthians with the assembly meeting in their house. Their hosting and coworking indicate meaningful agency within a mobile network of communities, but the greeting alone does not establish a fixed office, their wealth, or a permanent pattern of authority within their marriage.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:19"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_5a7e2dd94cf9760a84ee4418"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"A Connected Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Corinth lay on the isthmus between Cenchreae on the eastern side and Lechaeum on the western side, linking sea routes with north-south land travel. That setting makes the chapter’s repeated concern with journeys, visitors, greetings, and moving a gift more concrete.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:1-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_8693b14e71a1c0b93c5eadd8"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"The Closing Words","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"“Maranatha” is an Aramaic invocation preserved inside this Greek letter, commonly understood as “Our Lord, come.” Its division into words is debated, so speculative end-times timetables do not follow from it. The preceding anathema is severe boundary language, but it cannot authorize violence, forced conversion, harassment, or treating questioners as enemies.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:1-4","1 Corinthians 16:22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_3047040c59592636ba6d63cd"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Strength Governed by Love","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"The commands to watch, stand firm, act courageously, and be strong are immediately governed by the instruction to do everything in love. Read together with the holy-kiss greeting and the final words of grace and affection, this resists treating spiritual strength as intimidation or control. A greeting involving touch should be freely chosen and allow an unpenalized alternative or refusal.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 16:13-14","1 Corinthians 16:20","1 Corinthians 16:22-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_2970b547818a0d38e09f9b93"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Letter’s Transmission","blocks":[{"id":"block_9","text":"The wider letter survives in manuscripts that preserve different portions and textual variants; no single surviving witness simply is the original handwritten copy. Proposals that parts of the letter were added or divided should therefore be assessed passage by passage, rather than inferred from an abrupt transition or from theological preference alone.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_016_1c55c40655df7514094b1e17"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}